WEBVTT

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:09.220
Hi everybody, Cheryl Ackerson here.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.420
Welcome to another edition of the Cheryl Ackerson Podcast.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:19.340
Today a fascinating discussion with a former CIA officer, John Kiriakou, the only person

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:25.780
prosecuted and imprisoned over the CIA torture program not for taking part in it, but for

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.940
blowing the whistle on it.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:32.260
He will also talk about a new internet product you can use to search the internet

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.340
without big brother keeping track.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:41.340
It's called Panquake, P-A-N-Q-U-A-K-E, and he'll talk about what happened to him and

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:50.860
what could very well happen to you with a weaponized government growing out of control.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:01:01.380
My name is John Kiriakou, K-I-R-I-A-K-O-U, and I am, what is my title?

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:07.340
I guess it's Brand Spokesman for Panquake.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:11.180
Where'd you get the name, by the way, Panquake?

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.660
You know, they talked about that a lot in the beginning.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:19.580
These people that are involved in the development of Panquake are far more creative than I

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:20.580
am.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:28.620
I'm just not creative in that way, and they wanted it to sort of give the feeling

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:35.380
of an earthquake in that it's shaking up the industry, and Pan meaning it covers the

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:42.940
whole spectrum and the whole world, which is what they're hoping to finally do, and yeah,

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:43.940
that's what they came up with.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:49.660
Let me read a little bit about, just to get you started on describing what's going

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:53.620
on from, I guess, a news release.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:02:00.700
It says that Julian Assange supporters take on Big Tech, release new anti-spying archiving

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:01.700
tool.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:07.180
It says supporters of WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange have just changed the balance of power

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:13.860
between internet users and Big Tech launching Panquake.me, a free app that protects users

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:19.260
from being spied on by web trackers and preserves the original web content.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.940
Okay, let's break it down.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.020
Let's assume we're talking to people like me, they may use the internet, know something

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:31.540
of it, but let's try to be fairly simple in terms of how we describe things.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:37.260
What is the idea behind the need for this tool or this site that you all have created?

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:38.260
Right.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:46.580
Well, the need is for users, and that could be just the everyday, you know, Joe or

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.980
Jane sitting at their computer.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:56.100
It could be journalists, it could be activists who just don't want to be tracked.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.980
And for me, as a user, that's the most important thing here.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:04.020
There were several things that were so important to the developers.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:10.300
One was transparency, and the code is open so anybody can see how this thing is set up.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:15.740
The second thing was privacy, and privacy and transparency are not mutually exclusive.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:20.860
You know, social media sites like Twitter and Facebook and Instagram and really all

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:24.420
of them, they collect your personal data.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:29.220
They collect your metadata, they know what you share, they know what you forward, and

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:32.260
then they package that information and they sell it.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:39.620
They sell it to other companies, but more sinisterly, they sell it to the FBI and

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.020
SA and CIA.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.180
How do we know that?

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:44.180
I believe you.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.980
How do we know that they have contracts with them that we're able to see or what?

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.060
Yeah, through the Freedom of Information Act, there was actually a statement by the FBI

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:57.180
just a few weeks ago in which they said that now instead of going to a court and asking

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:03.580
for a warrant or a court order to purchase metadata, I mean to get metadata, they just

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:08.900
purchased it directly from the social media company, which is perfectly legal because

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:14.260
when you open an account, like let's say when you first set up your Facebook account, the

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:20.220
terms and conditions include a provision that you do not own the data.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:26.060
So any website you go to, you know, any news article you read, they collect information

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.940
on that and then they package it for sale.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:40.220
So literally everything you do on the internet can be purchased by the FBI, NSA, CIA or frankly

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.020
anybody else.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:48.260
But if you've agreed to that, let's say you have a Facebook account and you visit things

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:52.500
outside of your new development, outside of the thing that you all have developed,

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.020
is Facebook tracking what you do when you're not logged into Facebook somehow or is

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.500
it only the stuff you're doing while you're logged in?

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.580
No, that's a great question.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:05.180
And you know, for a long time, people suspected that Facebook was tracking them continually

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:09.540
and finally Facebook came out just in the last year and said, yes, that they do actually

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:10.540
track us.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:16.340
For example, I was talking to a friend of mine on the phone.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:17.620
I was not on Facebook.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:23.300
I was just on the phone and I was telling him, oh, you know, I'm accosted by lunatics

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:29.660
all the time and, you know, there was this woman that came after me and she had fantasized

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:37.220
that we were engaged and she actually flew to Washington with a gun to shoot my wife

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:40.220
so that she and I could be together.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:42.660
And he said, oh, buddy, you know what you need?

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:44.620
You need a concealed carry class.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:48.220
And I said, yeah, but I'm not allowed to carry a gun.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:54.300
As soon as I hung up the phone, I went on Facebook and literally every single advertisement

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:58.100
was for a concealed carry course in my area.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.740
So yeah, isn't that awful?

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:04.580
So you know, it's like, it's like another example.

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:07.420
I told my sister, oh, I need to buy a new desk.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:08.500
I don't like my desk.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:09.500
It's really old.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:12.700
I want one that has, you know, a USB port.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:15.180
No sooner did I hang up the phone.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:21.060
I go into the Pittsburgh Post Gazette and every pop-up ad on the Pittsburgh Post Gazette

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.580
is for a computer desk.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.740
You know, that's just not acceptable.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:30.460
Many of us I think don't want to live our lives like that or we're tired of living

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:32.060
our lives like that.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:38.180
And we want to be able to surf the web or read an article or communicate with friends

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:43.660
or colleagues or journalists or whomever and just not be tracked.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:44.660
That's what this is.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:52.860
Before we go into some more details about Panquake or panquake.ne, it wouldn't stop that, would

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:53.860
it?

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:57.380
Like if you're, you have a Facebook account and they're tracking you and you're on the

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:02.660
phone, but you're not going through Panquake, Panquake can't impact that, can it?

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:04.440
No, that it won't impact.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:11.260
But what it can impact is if you want to send somebody an article, for example,

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:17.220
Now if you see an article on Facebook and you open it, Facebook knows you've opened it.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:22.940
They know how long you were in it to read it and then they know if you sent it to somebody.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:27.060
Once you send it to that person, they know if that person opened it, how long they

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:30.020
read it and what they did with it.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:37.980
What you can do at Panquake is you can go into the site.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:41.060
It's something called a link shortener, right?

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:49.540
So you go to pnqk.me, you put in the URL there, you're able to read the article, it cleans

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:56.220
your links of any marketing or ad tracking cookies, you're able to send it to somebody

00:07:56.220 --> 00:08:02.340
else through the Panquake site and then it actually archives the link.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:07.660
One of the things that I hate and I'm sure that you do too because you're a prominent

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:12.420
journalist and you've done it for a long time, is you'll use something as a source

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:15.220
and then the link goes dead.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:19.420
And so you go back to it a year, two years, five years later

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.660
because you want to refer to it and the link no longer works.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:30.100
But Panquake will archive the link so that it will always be available to you.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:35.460
As long as you save it in the new, shortened, translated version that you got.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:38.380
Right, which the site does for you.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:39.980
Cool. Yeah.

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:42.020
Well, I think one we should discuss.

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:47.940
One of the things people ought to consider today is it's one thing.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:50.180
I guess it's less nefarious in a way.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:54.300
People are trying to advertise, send their products to you, listen in on you and

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:57.300
track you to send you advertising.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:00.820
Not that that's good, but you agree to that and that's the presumed

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:02.420
correct goal.

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:06.860
But we now have learned so much about what the government is doing.

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:12.420
So the government can access this data in many different ways and use it against

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:15.740
you in ways you never imagined, even if you've done nothing wrong.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:17.820
Oh, my God, that's so true.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:22.340
Find out if you happen to be in a city like Washington, D.C.

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:25.500
on January 6th, even if you broke no laws or did nothing wrong.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:26.100
Amen.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:28.940
You may end up by your behavior, being able to be tracked there and

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:35.540
targeted unfairly by this, you know, weaponized institutions, the institutions

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:39.500
we now have that are so politically weaponized.

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:42.140
You're absolutely right.

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:43.580
Absolutely right.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:49.940
You know, companies or the government can

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:53.740
entail a lot just by your metadata.

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:54.100
Right.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:56.100
They don't have to interview you.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:02.980
They don't have to put a team of spies to surveil you and track you just from your

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:08.100
metadata, they can get a pretty darn good view of what you're doing on a

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:10.180
day to day basis and how you're living your life.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:15.980
Like you say, even with your phone off or in airplane mode, they can still

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:19.540
track you to within three feet of your location.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:25.220
And besides that, what if you what if you are, you know,

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:32.500
chatting with, let's say, an abortion clinic or a boyfriend or girlfriend

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:37.700
or, you know, a psychiatrist or psychologist.

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:40.980
Don't you think you deserve a little bit of privacy?

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:41.780
I think so.

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:45.340
And not that they deserve to butt into your life anyway,

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:50.420
but the conclusions they may mistakenly draw from your histories as a

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:54.260
journalist, I sometimes look for

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:55.860
incorrect information.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:59.180
You know, one of my big gripes about all the censorship is sometimes I want to

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:03.860
see what people are saying that may not be right or may be right, but other people

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:04.860
are trying to disparage it.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:08.300
I don't want a third party divining truth for me.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:12.380
And by the same token, and I may be researching predators, but you could

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:17.460
look at the search history for me and wrongly assume that I am a predator.

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:18.380
There's all kinds of.

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:19.460
Oh, yeah.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:22.980
But the government peeking into your private life, of course.

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:25.460
Oh, you're absolutely right.

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:29.380
Can you imagine how potentially dangerous that is?

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:30.340
It's just awful.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:34.100
For me, because of my background, I'm really worried about the national

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:38.540
security state gathering and then misusing this kind of information.

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:40.940
But you make a very important point right there.

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:45.380
It's not just, you know, John has a beef with the national security state.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:50.860
It's that every single American is potentially at risk.

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:53.740
Tell me a little bit about your background.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:57.140
Sure, I spent 15 years with the CIA.

00:11:57.340 --> 00:12:00.260
The first half was as an analyst on the Middle East.

00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:04.700
The second half was in counterterrorism operations.

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:10.220
I rose quickly at the CIA and I became the chief of counterterrorism

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:13.700
operations in Pakistan after 9-11.

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:18.940
I led a team of counterterrorism

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:23.220
officers in a series of raids in March of 2002 that resulted in the capture

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:27.460
of Abu Zubaydah, who we believed at the time was the number three in al-Qaeda.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:29.460
He was not the number three.

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:33.020
The CIA just a few months later began torturing Abu Zubaydah.

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:36.660
I went back to CIA headquarters and became the executive

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:39.940
assistant to the CIA's deputy director for operations.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:43.260
And I objected to the torture program.

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:47.860
Not only did I object to it, I refused to be trained in it.

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:52.700
I was the only person of the 14 approached to be interrogators to refuse

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:58.580
the torture training, which, as you might imagine, damaged my career.

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:00.500
But

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:04.340
but I didn't care and I waited for somebody to come out and say something

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:08.180
because this torture program was so patently illegal to me.

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:13.260
I just expected that somebody would come out and say something that never happened.

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:16.900
And so five and a half years later in 2007,

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:20.980
I said something in an interview with Brian Ross at ABC News.

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:23.500
I said that CIA was torturing its prisoners,

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:28.380
that torture was official US government policy and that the policy had been

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:31.100
personally approved by the president.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:35.700
As you might imagine, the CIA filed a crimes report against me with the FBI.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:37.980
And the FBI investigated me for a year

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:42.580
from December 2007 to December 2008, and they determined

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:46.660
at the end of that year that I had not committed a crime and they closed the case.

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:49.820
So three weeks later, Barack Obama becomes president.

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:51.580
John Brennan becomes the deputy

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:54.500
national security adviser for counterterrorism.

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:56.460
John was an old nemesis of mine.

00:13:56.660 --> 00:13:59.620
And John was one of the fathers of the torture program.

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:04.740
And so he asked the Justice Department to secretly reopen the case against me.

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:07.300
I had no idea that for the next three years,

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:10.380
and this is why Panquake is so important to me, the next three years,

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:13.740
my phones were tapped, my emails were being intercepted,

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:16.980
FBI agents were following me everywhere I went.

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:22.940
And then in January of 2012, I was charged with five felonies,

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:28.100
including three counts of espionage for speaking to ABC and to The New York Times.

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:30.420
Of course, I hadn't committed espionage

00:14:30.620 --> 00:14:33.140
and the espionage charges were dropped.

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:38.700
But but I finally took a plea to to violating the Intelligence Identity's

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:42.100
Protection Act of 1982.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:45.940
I'm only the second American that was ever charged with that crime.

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:49.060
And I did it to make the whole thing go away.

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:50.580
I'm proud to say that in the end,

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:53.980
I was educated because everything I said was true.

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:58.620
I had great support from the likes of of John McCain, for example.

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:02.180
But but I served 23 months in a federal prison

00:15:02.420 --> 00:15:08.220
and came out with a new appreciation for for privacy on the one hand

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:10.820
and for government transparency on the other.

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:17.140
And then when when Susie Dawson and this incredible group of

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:20.980
tech geniuses, I don't even know what else to call them because, you know,

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:23.500
we have these calls and they're talking about the system.

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:25.780
And I'm like, God bless them because I don't know what the heck they're

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:27.620
talking about, it's so sophisticated.

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:30.300
But then at the same time, it's so simple.

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:33.980
You know that the the technology behind it is sophisticated.

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:38.620
But the the ease of use is such that

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:41.060
anybody can use it. I'm older than you are.

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:45.780
I'm not a Luddite, but but, you know, I don't want to get too down into the

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:48.020
weeds and you don't have to with this.

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:53.500
So anyway, I have this appreciation for transparency and and privacy.

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:56.140
And this sort of puts it all in one package for me.

00:15:56.340 --> 00:16:00.340
That's why I agreed to to join up with them.

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:05.930
I think they have an amazing idea and I'd like to be a part of it.

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:11.490
Just to digress for a moment, when the FBI was following you around

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:15.130
and your phones were being tapped and all that stuff,

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:16.490
were you aware of it?

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:20.130
Were there signs or signals or was something you found out about in retrospect?

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:23.290
No, I had no idea.

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:27.770
And I'll tell you what, I was a surveillance detection instructor.

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:34.930
At the CIA and I never bothered to look because I hadn't done anything wrong.

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:39.970
And so I had no idea that I was being followed.

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:44.930
I had no idea that my phone calls and emails were being intercepted.

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:48.410
And then just like you said a moment ago,

00:16:48.610 --> 00:16:54.010
they'll take these these emails completely out of context

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:57.250
and then try to use it against you.

00:16:57.450 --> 00:16:59.090
And that happened to me repeatedly.

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:02.170
That happened to a

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:07.330
gentleman, a professor who had been accused of being a Chinese spy by the FBI.

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:08.890
Yeah, when?

00:17:09.090 --> 00:17:12.250
Well, him too, but actually there's a whole series of them.

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:16.730
And there's one more recent with an ongoing case he has against the FBI.

